WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.810 --> 00:00:15.280
You know this scene from The Wizard of Oz.

00:00:15.280 --> 00:00:19.270
It happens just after Dorothy croons in sepia-toned
Kansas,

00:00:19.270 --> 00:00:24.679
Toto wags his tail,
and the house gets caught in a tornado.

00:00:24.679 --> 00:00:29.579
She travels from a faded film strip to a Technicolor
world.

00:00:29.579 --> 00:00:32.990
But there are three things about this scene
you might get wrong.

00:00:32.990 --> 00:00:37.210
And each one helps show the real history of
Technicolor.

00:00:37.210 --> 00:00:42.040
These misconceptions explain what the
“Technicolor triumph” really was, from

00:00:42.040 --> 00:00:47.710
the technical aspects that made it work, to
exactly why it took over the movies, to the

00:00:47.710 --> 00:00:55.600
way in which the technology shaped the look
of the 20th century.

00:00:55.600 --> 00:01:03.440
Lie #1 - Wizard of Oz is not the first Technicolor
movie.

00:01:03.440 --> 00:01:06.610
Not even close.

00:01:06.610 --> 00:01:09.249
You might know that, but a lot of people don’t.

00:01:09.249 --> 00:01:13.460
Come on Maryland Science Center, you’re
better than this.

00:01:13.460 --> 00:01:18.259
Historian Barbara Flueckiger has an exhaustive
timeline of color in film, from

00:01:18.259 --> 00:01:21.240
hand-painted film
to the first movie filmed in “kinemacolor,”

00:01:21.240 --> 00:01:23.929
A Visit to the Seaside.

00:01:23.929 --> 00:01:28.770
But Technicolor stood out, and even it has
a history that long predates The Wizard of

00:01:28.770 --> 00:01:29.770
Oz.

00:01:29.770 --> 00:01:36.700
Herbert Kalmus, Daniel Frost Comstock, and
W. Burton Wescott founded the company in 1914,

00:01:36.700 --> 00:01:41.960
with the “Tech” referring to MIT, where
Kalmus and Comstock met.

00:01:41.960 --> 00:01:49.149
It started by merging red and green - into
a new image that roughly looked like this.

00:01:49.149 --> 00:01:55.229
You can see the look in this range of movies
from the late 1920s and early 30s.

00:01:55.229 --> 00:01:59.460
It could do passably well with skin tones,
but

00:01:59.460 --> 00:02:03.049
there’s no blue in these dresses for a reason.

00:02:03.049 --> 00:02:09.440
Blue came into the mix in 1932, when Technicolor
added the key third strip.

00:02:09.440 --> 00:02:15.220
They showed off the process in Walt Disney’s
Flowers and Trees, a gorgeous animated feature

00:02:15.220 --> 00:02:16.730
that was a botanist’s nightmare.

00:02:16.730 --> 00:02:19.959
You know, there are evil trees in Wizard of
Oz, too.

00:02:19.959 --> 00:02:22.180
“What do you think you’re doing?”

00:02:22.180 --> 00:02:27.700
Anyway, in order to get Technicolor to work,
it was an insanely difficult process.

00:02:27.700 --> 00:02:29.860
Technicolor distributed
guides like these

00:02:29.860 --> 00:02:34.180
and we can make a reasonable simulation digitally,
with a scene like this.

00:02:34.180 --> 00:02:38.840
So here’s a scene of some Lego people who
are apparently worshipping Lawrence of Arabia?

00:02:38.840 --> 00:02:41.319
Not sure what’s going on here, but it’s
our starting image.

00:02:41.319 --> 00:02:45.970
A technicolor camera would typically take
that picture and shoot it through a prism

00:02:45.970 --> 00:02:50.409
that split the light into red, blue, and green
negatives for the picture.

00:02:50.409 --> 00:02:55.439
Those negatives were then flipped into positive
“matrices,”

00:02:55.439 --> 00:02:59.890
which eventually got soaked with dyes of the
complementary colors.

00:02:59.890 --> 00:03:06.400
So the red matrix turned cyan, the green one
magenta, and the blue one yellow.

00:03:06.400 --> 00:03:11.760
Then the dye was transferred — this was
called a “dye transfer process” to create

00:03:11.760 --> 00:03:14.390
a final gorgeous Technicolor image.

00:03:14.390 --> 00:03:17.330
So if you’re anything like me, that explanation
might make you feel like the scarecrow.

00:03:17.330 --> 00:03:21.000
“Oh I’m a failure because I haven’t
got a brain.”

00:03:21.000 --> 00:03:23.450
So let’s try it again, but only look at
that red channel.

00:03:23.450 --> 00:03:28.579
So keep your eye on the View-Master, the red
in the Rubik’s cube, or maybe the Lego guy’s

00:03:28.579 --> 00:03:29.579
hat.

00:03:29.579 --> 00:03:34.849
It is all kind of dark now, because that’s
just the red color in the negative.

00:03:34.849 --> 00:03:40.840
Now flipped in the matrix, that red is really
bright, which means that when it’s dyed,

00:03:40.840 --> 00:03:42.680
it won’t get a lot of
cyan.

00:03:42.680 --> 00:03:44.439
And that makes sense.

00:03:44.439 --> 00:03:48.769
Cyan is the complementary color — it’s
the anti-red.

00:03:48.769 --> 00:03:53.189
So where you want a lot of red, you do not
want a lot of cyan.

00:03:53.189 --> 00:03:57.780
That way, when it comes together, you get
a ton of magenta and some yellow.

00:03:57.780 --> 00:04:03.170
You don’t have a lot of cyan, because the
cyan cancels out the red.

00:04:03.170 --> 00:04:07.439
In the earlier days of Technicolor, they also
had to amp up the contrast.

00:04:07.439 --> 00:04:11.749
The company would add a black and white layer
underneath the matrices to serve as something

00:04:11.749 --> 00:04:13.310
called "the key."

00:04:13.310 --> 00:04:18.000
You can see the results early, in films like
1934’s La Cucaracha,

00:04:18.000 --> 00:04:22.610
The Trail of the Lonesome Pine,
and Robin Hood, all of which came out well

00:04:22.610 --> 00:04:25.920
before the Wizard of Oz.

00:04:25.920 --> 00:04:33.320
It’s easy enough to roughly copy the technology
that “Technicolored” the Wizard of Oz.

00:04:33.320 --> 00:04:36.889
RGB split, color bath, mesh, repeat.

00:04:36.889 --> 00:04:41.639
But the film strip processes are just part
of the story.

00:04:41.639 --> 00:04:44.220
Lie #2: this scene?

00:04:44.220 --> 00:04:50.650
It’s not going from a black and white world
to a color one.

00:04:50.650 --> 00:04:55.880
The set was actually painted sepia-tone so
the same Technicolor process could be used

00:04:55.880 --> 00:04:57.540
for the bright Oz reveal.

00:04:57.540 --> 00:04:59.229
Today, it’s much easier.

00:04:59.229 --> 00:05:05.150
I can draw a box with my hand and with a click,
black and white and color play together.

00:05:05.150 --> 00:05:08.479
They even had techniques to do stuff like
this in the Oz days.

00:05:08.479 --> 00:05:14.520
But the fact that they built a sepia house
shows how Technicolor’s technical limitations

00:05:14.520 --> 00:05:19.979
shaped all color movies.

00:05:19.979 --> 00:05:25.389
“This is one of the cameras that was used
to film

00:05:25.389 --> 00:05:26.389
The Wizard of Oz.”

00:05:26.389 --> 00:05:32.030
“It weighs 4 to 500 pounds, and these cameras
were bigger than ordinary motion picture cameras

00:05:32.030 --> 00:05:35.590
because they had to run three strips of film
through them at any given time.”

00:05:35.590 --> 00:05:37.639
So remember — this scene?

00:05:37.639 --> 00:05:41.220
That had to be done with this beast of a camera.

00:05:41.220 --> 00:05:45.620
Those three strips didn’t just require more
space, they needed tons of light.

00:05:45.620 --> 00:05:50.110
That set had to be blazingly overlit to get
enough light through to these three strips

00:05:50.110 --> 00:05:51.110
of film.

00:05:51.110 --> 00:05:55.380
The set was reportedly 100 degrees Fahrenheit
at times.

00:05:55.380 --> 00:05:56.590
Sound was an issue, too.

00:05:56.590 --> 00:06:00.280
“It’s so loud when you’re running three
strips of film through a camera, so they had

00:06:00.280 --> 00:06:02.020
to build this blimp around it.

00:06:02.020 --> 00:06:05.030
It’s filled with soundproofing material
so when you’re making a sound film you don’t

00:06:05.030 --> 00:06:08.870
get all the sound from the camera throughout
the studio there.”

00:06:08.870 --> 00:06:11.139
Technicolor’s advantages outweighed its
limitations.

00:06:11.139 --> 00:06:15.770
It’s main advantage was the way in which
it could capture the tone of a scene.

00:06:15.770 --> 00:06:19.379
Two movies made in the same year could have
a different look, not just because of the

00:06:19.379 --> 00:06:22.110
choices made in front of the camera.

00:06:22.110 --> 00:06:27.300
Technicolor consultants and directors tweaked
the palette of the film by adjusting the cyan,

00:06:27.300 --> 00:06:28.930
magenta and yellow dyes.

00:06:28.930 --> 00:06:33.660
The complicated dye transfer process gave
Singin’ in the Rain some of its magenta-hued

00:06:33.660 --> 00:06:36.629
skin and deep saturated colors.

00:06:36.629 --> 00:06:40.240
The film and technology weren’t the only
things that gave Technicolor movies their

00:06:40.240 --> 00:06:41.319
distinctive look.

00:06:41.319 --> 00:06:46.810
It also shaped the world that they chose to
film.

00:06:46.810 --> 00:06:49.789
Lie #3: This isn’t the real Dorothy.

00:06:49.789 --> 00:06:54.220
It’s Judy Garland’s body double.

00:06:54.220 --> 00:06:59.000
She wore specially designed clothes and makeup
to match the sepia world, so Judy Garland

00:06:59.000 --> 00:07:08.330
could swoop in, in the same shot and
a blue dress, to join Technicolor Oz.

00:07:08.330 --> 00:07:13.430
These movies, and Oz, were shaped around Technicolor’s
abilities, from head to toe.

00:07:13.430 --> 00:07:17.940
“The second page that you see here is the
part of the script that shows the ruby slippers

00:07:17.940 --> 00:07:22.099
being unveiled, but what it shows is that
they were still silver shoes at this point,

00:07:22.099 --> 00:07:25.050
but the producers of the film really wanted
to show off that Technicolor that they were

00:07:25.050 --> 00:07:28.610
paying for, so they wanted them to be sparkly
ruby slippers that would look good against

00:07:28.610 --> 00:07:29.610
the yellow brick road.

00:07:29.610 --> 00:07:32.229
So they changed it at the middle of production
to ruby slippers.”

00:07:32.229 --> 00:07:36.000
Today, the shoes are kept under low light
to preserve them, but during the shoot they

00:07:36.000 --> 00:07:40.729
were blasted with light to accommodate the
camera and make those sequins sparkle.

00:07:40.729 --> 00:07:46.300
These weren’t just on-set decisions — Technicolor
was always pulling strings behind the curtains.

00:07:46.300 --> 00:07:48.289
Look at the credits for Wizard of Oz,

00:07:48.289 --> 00:07:53.240
and The Trail of the Lonesome Pine, and A
Star is Born, and so on and so on.

00:07:53.240 --> 00:07:56.319
You’ll see one name over and over.

00:07:56.319 --> 00:07:57.789
Natalie Kalmus.

00:07:57.789 --> 00:08:03.069
Once married to Technicolor cofounder Herb
Kalmus, she ruled with an iron fist over

00:08:03.069 --> 00:08:06.419
Technicolor productions for many of the early
years.

00:08:06.419 --> 00:08:11.849
Kalmus had over 300 film credits where she
gave Technicolor advice — and sometimes

00:08:11.849 --> 00:08:14.639
told directors what to do.

00:08:14.639 --> 00:08:18.860
This is the IMDB page for a woman born in
1882.

00:08:18.860 --> 00:08:27.180
In 
documents like “Color Consciousness,”

00:08:27.180 --> 00:08:32.479
she extended her reach into art — the essay
includes aesthetic color theory.

00:08:32.479 --> 00:08:36.390
“Red: danger, blood, life, heat.

00:08:36.390 --> 00:08:40.290
Green: Nature, outdoors, freedom, freshness.”

00:08:40.290 --> 00:08:47.270
Kalmus’s influence was significant, but
it’s as important as a reflection of Technicolor’s

00:08:47.270 --> 00:08:48.270
power.

00:08:48.270 --> 00:08:52.950
Technicolor had its own processing facilities,
and its own camera crew that continued Natalie

00:08:52.950 --> 00:08:55.670
Kalmus’ work after she left the company.

00:08:55.670 --> 00:08:59.950
The technology and the production process
gave Technicolor a significant competitive

00:08:59.950 --> 00:09:02.930
advantage to alternatives being used.

00:09:02.930 --> 00:09:08.160
Despite all those alternatives shown on Barbara
Flueckiger’s website, studios stuck with

00:09:08.160 --> 00:09:10.000
Technicolor for a long time.

00:09:10.000 --> 00:09:14.760
It had a reliable system and
could be shown in any theatre in splendid

00:09:14.760 --> 00:09:17.339
color, without requiring special equipment.

00:09:17.339 --> 00:09:23.560
Technicolor eventually fell to cheaper processes
through the 1950s, like Eastman Color, that

00:09:23.560 --> 00:09:24.610
used a single strip.

00:09:24.610 --> 00:09:30.790
The Godfather, Part II was one of the final
major releases to use the Technicolor we recognize.

00:09:30.790 --> 00:09:36.540
But old prints remain surprisingly vibrant
today due to the dye transfer process used.

00:09:36.540 --> 00:09:41.740
Today, I can snap my fingers and be in
The Matrix or in Stranger Things’ Upside

00:09:41.740 --> 00:09:42.860
Down.

00:09:42.860 --> 00:09:43.980
Ok.

00:09:43.980 --> 00:09:46.390
What are all these dust particles?

00:09:46.390 --> 00:09:47.390
Is this asbestos?

00:09:47.390 --> 00:09:52.850
Am I covered in asbestos right now?

00:09:52.850 --> 00:09:57.050
Technicolor was never just a click —
the look was formed by the camera’s strengths

00:09:57.050 --> 00:10:02.180
and weaknesses, the artistic choices made
for color, and the Technicolor company’s

00:10:02.180 --> 00:10:04.350
infrastructure and supervision.

00:10:04.350 --> 00:10:08.941
In that key scene from the Wizard of Oz, you
might not have known the trivia about Dorothy’s

00:10:08.941 --> 00:10:14.510
double, or the sepia doorway, or even that
it wasn’t Technicolor’s debut.

00:10:14.510 --> 00:10:18.259
But one thing is easy to understand, intuitively.

00:10:18.259 --> 00:10:20.740
The movie is all about it.

00:10:20.740 --> 00:10:23.130
Technicolor wasn’t a switch or a doorway.

00:10:23.130 --> 00:10:36.310
It was a whole world, just waiting on the
other side.

00:10:36.310 --> 00:10:40.920
You can nerd out a lot more on Technicolor
by checking out Barbara Flueckiger’s website,

00:10:40.920 --> 00:10:46.190
or Eastman House, which was really generous
with their time and a lot of the images that

00:10:46.190 --> 00:10:47.190
you saw in this video.

00:10:47.190 --> 00:10:49.070
I’ve linked both of those below.

00:10:49.070 --> 00:10:54.410
You can see the director’s commentary for
this video in an additional video that we’ve

00:10:54.410 --> 00:11:00.019
made where I share some behind-the-scenes
info and a few of the details that couldn’t

00:11:00.019 --> 00:11:00.730
quite fit in.

